WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.765
Successful local inference depends on a few core terms.

00:00:03.765 --> 00:00:11.296
A model file contains the data needed to run a trained model, commonly in GGUF format for llama.cpp workflows.

00:00:11.296 --> 00:00:18.621
The tokenizer converts text into tokens the model can process and converts generated tokens back into text.

00:00:18.621 --> 00:00:29.780
A prompt is the input you provide, while the context window is the amount of prompt, conversation history, and supporting text the model can consider during a run.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:34.161
Prompt processing and token generation are two different phases.

00:00:34.161 --> 00:00:39.159
Prompt processing is the work of reading and preparing the input context.

00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:42.992
Token generation is the step-by-step creation of output.

00:00:42.992 --> 00:00:48.058
A setup may process short prompts quickly but slow down with long context.

00:00:48.058 --> 00:00:57.916
It may load a model successfully but generate slowly because the chosen model, quantization, hardware, or runtime settings are not well matched.

00:00:57.916 --> 00:01:00.313
Sampling settings shape the output.

00:01:00.313 --> 00:01:06.885
Temperature and related controls affect how deterministic, varied, or creative the result feels.

00:01:06.885 --> 00:01:10.102
Output length limits prevent runaway responses.

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:14.141
Stop tokens help the runtime know when to end a completion.

00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:22.493
These settings do not make a weak model strong, but they can make a suitable model behave poorly if configured carelessly.

00:01:22.493 --> 00:01:28.107
CPU inference, GPU acceleration, and quantization are practical operating choices.

00:01:28.107 --> 00:01:32.899
CPU inference can be simple and portable but slower for larger models.

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:38.924
GPU acceleration can improve speed when supported by hardware and runtime configuration.

00:01:38.924 --> 00:01:45.564
Quantization reduces memory requirements and file size, often with quality or behavior tradeoffs.

00:01:45.564 --> 00:01:52.547
Local inference works best when model, runtime, hardware, and configuration are treated as one system.
